    <section class="pad" id="rig">
      <div class="inner">
        <div class="sechead">
          <span class="eyebrow">03 · Capture</span>
          <h2>The rig turns the knobs so nobody has to</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="cols">
          <div class="prose">
            <p>A full-range capture grid is too large for human hands: tens of thousands of states, each needing a settled, repeatable control position before a note is recorded. So we built an actuation rig. Stepper motors couple directly to each control through machined adapters, home against the physical end stops, and step through the grid overnight while the capture chain records.</p>
            <p>One detail matters more than the motors: <strong>knob angle is not the measurement.</strong> Potentiometer tapers drift and vary between units, so the rig logs the resulting electrical behavior at every position and the model trains against that. The angle is just the index. The behavior is the ground truth.</p>
          </div>
          <figure class="figure">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" role="img" aria-label="Family of saturation transfer curves at increasing drive">
              <rect x="0" y="0" width="460" height="250" fill="#0b0d0c"/>
              <g stroke="#242b27" stroke-width="1">
                <line x1="50" y1="125" x2="430" y2="125"/>
                <line x1="240" y1="20" x2="240" y2="230"/>
              </g>
              <text x="432" y="118" fill="#8d978f" font-size="10" text-anchor="end">IN</text>
              <text x="248" y="30" fill="#8d978f" font-size="10">OUT</text>
              <path d="M 60 190 C 150 170, 200 150, 240 125 C 280 100, 330 80, 420 60" fill="none" stroke="#1f7a3c" stroke-width="1.6"/>
              <path d="M 60 203 C 140 185, 205 155, 240 125 C 275 95, 340 65, 420 47" fill="none" stroke="#2a9c50" stroke-width="1.6"/>
              <path d="M 60 214 C 130 200, 208 160, 240 125 C 272 90, 350 50, 420 36" fill="none" stroke="#35c75f" stroke-width="1.8"/>
              <path d="M 60 222 C 120 213, 212 165, 240 125 C 268 85, 360 37, 420 28" fill="none" stroke="#41e86e" stroke-width="2"/>
              <text x="60" y="243" fill="#8d978f" font-size="10">measured transfer curves · one control sweep, 41 steps (4 of 41 shown)</text>
            </svg>
            <figcaption>Fig. 2 — Drive sweep, measured at the output transformer</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="speclist">
          <div class="spec"><div class="k">Coupling</div><div class="v">Direct stepper-to-shaft, machined adapters. No grippers, no camera guesswork.</div></div>
          <div class="spec"><div class="k">Repeatability</div><div class="v">Homed against end stops; position repeatable to a fraction of a degree across the full campaign.</div></div>
          <div class="spec"><div class="k">Grid design</div><div class="v">Dense per-control sweeps plus randomized cross-combinations to learn control interactions.</div></div>
          <div class="spec"><div class="k">Campaign</div><div class="v">14 hours unattended per unit. Switch states set by hand between sweep passes.</div></div>
          <div class="spec"><div class="k">Verification</div><div class="v">5% of states re-captured at the end of each run and diffed against the first pass.</div></div>
          <div class="spec"><div class="k">Signal set</div><div class="v">Swept sines, stepped tones, pink bursts, and program material: drums, bass, vocal, mix bus.</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pad" id="accuracy">
      <div class="inner">
        <div class="sechead">
          <span class="eyebrow">04 · Accuracy</span>
          <h2>Judged by cancellation, not adjectives</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="lede">Every build is validated the same way: the plugin and the hardware process the same audio at matched settings, one is inverted, and the two are summed. What's left is the residual — everything the model got wrong. We publish the numbers because the method only counts if you can check it.</p>

        <div class="meter">
          <div class="row"><div class="name">Program material · mix bus</div><div class="bar"><i class="deep" style="width:86%"></i></div><div class="val">−62 dB</div></div>
          <div class="row"><div class="name">Drums · high drive</div><div class="bar"><i class="deep" style="width:82%"></i></div><div class="val">−58 dB</div></div>
          <div class="row"><div class="name">Bass · transformer region</div><div class="bar"><i style="width:78%"></i></div><div class="val">−55 dB</div></div>
          <div class="row"><div class="name">Swept sine · worst case</div><div class="bar"><i style="width:74%"></i></div><div class="val">−52 dB</div></div>
        </div>
        <p class="footnote">Validation targets for release 1.0, measured as RMS residual against the reference unit at 96 kHz. Figures shown are the targets our null-test suite enforces in CI; a build that misses them doesn't ship. Error-to-signal ratio (ESR) per capture state is logged in full and will be published with the release notes.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pad">
      <div class="inner">
        <div class="sechead">
          <span class="eyebrow">05 · Presets</span>
          <h2>Three banks, named for the downlink</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="lede">Factory presets are organized the way satellite engineers talk about amplifiers, because it turns out they have the same problems we do.</p>
